REFRACTORY DEBT

The post-burst unresolved population
is not primarily an opposing commitment.

In the echo arms,
the opposing committed fraction
is only approximately 6-7%
at burst end
and then falls further during support recovery.

The dominant unresolved state
is refractory FC / FS:

approximately 45-47%
of the whole population
at A45 ECHO_PHASE burst end.

Current refractory mechanics are:

when a committed cell defects,
it enters side-specific FC or FS
with timer D.

D2:
D = 2.

D3:
D = 3.

While FC/FS,
the cell is externally unresolved
and cannot participate in U recruitment.

Only when the timer expires
does it return to U.

The favored majority reappears
on approximately the same timescale
as the refractory mass releases.

CAUSAL INTERPRETATION

A45 changed the residual problem
more than the inherited oscillator score reveals.

The strengthened core does not
flip to the opposing side.

Instead,
the adverse burst ejects
a large population into refractory debt.

Those cells are prevented
from recommitting during
the early support-recovery interval.

The organism therefore loses
its favored majority temporarily
even though:

the opposing committed population is tiny;

the surviving favored core remains stronger
than in A44;

and the environment has returned
to favored support.

This produces repeated:

favored -> unresolved -> favored

majority-label changes,
which the inherited detector
counts as oscillation.

SCIENTIFIC CONSEQUENCE

Two separate residual questions
must now be tracked.

1. WRONG-SIDE INSTABILITY

Does the organism
actually switch majority
to the adverse side?

In the audited frozen 5:3 A45 worlds:

NO post-epoch-8 adverse-majority entry
was observed.

2. UNCERTAINTY / RECOVERY CYCLING

Does the organism
temporarily lose majority
into unresolved states
and take multiple support epochs
to recover?

YES.

This remains potentially important
for service continuity
and developmental stability,
but it is a different failure mode
from pulse-chasing into the wrong commitment.

METRIC DISCIPLINE GOING FORWARD

Do not discard
the original oscillator metric.

Preserve it for lineage comparability.

But all successor experiments
must additionally report:

direct favored-to-adverse majority flips;

adverse-majority entries;

adverse-majority epochs;

favored-to-unresolved transitions;

unresolved-to-favored transitions;

unresolved episode duration;

refractory occupancy;

favored-majority recovery latency.

A successor must not claim
that it solved harmful oscillation
merely by reducing
favored/unresolved transitions.

NEXT DIAGNOSTIC BEFORE A46

Before changing refractory behavior,
measure refractory cells
during the early support-recovery interval.

For FC/FS cells,
report:

former commitment side;

current signed-B support
for that former side;

current local SUPPORT / CHALLENGE / NEUTRAL mode
relative to that former side;

remaining refractory timer;

local same-side support field;

whether release to U
would occur under candidate evidence gates.

Run the same observational gate audit
in:

5:3 recovery;

balanced-periodic contexts;

true-reversal contexts;

and noisy/nonperiodic controls.

The purpose is to determine
whether an evidence-aligned early release rule
can distinguish:

temporary counter-pulse recovery

from:

genuine reversal
or balanced noise.

No refractory change
is authorized by this diagnostic.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

We found something important.

The system is not actually
changing its mind back and forth
between the two sides.

After the stronger A45 core
takes the three-hit attack,
almost nobody joins
the wrong side.

Instead,
a huge chunk of the population
is sitting on the bench.

They recently defected,
so the refractory rule
forces them to wait
before they can become recruitable again.

During that waiting period
the favored side temporarily
does not have a majority.

Then the timers expire,
those cells re-enter the recruitable pool,
and the favored majority comes back.

Our old oscillator detector
counts that as another oscillation.

So the next problem is more precise:

not

HOW DO WE STOP THE POPULATION
FROM CHANGING SIDES?

A45 already appears
to have prevented that
in these 5:3 worlds.

The question is now:

HOW DO WE LET
RECENTLY DISLODGED CELLS
RETURN TO PARTICIPATION SOONER

WHEN LOCAL EVIDENCE
CLEARLY SAYS THE OLD SIDE
IS SUPPORTED AGAIN,

WITHOUT MAKING THEM CHATTER
DURING NOISE
OR REFUSE A REAL REVERSAL?

We should measure that signal
before changing the refractory rule.

NORTH-STAR IMPLICATION

This is a meaningful distinction
for developmental intelligence.

A robust distributed organism
may legitimately enter uncertainty
under disturbance
without adopting the wrong organization.

That is different from pathological oscillation.

The remaining design question
is now recovery control:

how quickly locally displaced cells
should become plastic again
after the disturbance passes.

